aim that is that it's an  unusual claim so the reason I did the  book is that I want to say something  that's that's not been said a thousand  times before the claim is that the  navigation problem is a freedom problem  there's a debate about which there are  many thousands of pages spilled probably  every month which is about the most of  them thank goodness are not published  about the positive and negative freedom  debate my view about that is that it has  a large semantic dimension which makes  it less interesting where the advocates  of positive freedom claim freedom  entails or right to things and the  advocates of negative freedom say  freedom entails right freedom  from impositions of various sorts this  on my view has a definitional character  which means what's underlying it  substantively is is not that clear  clearly the Friends of positive freedom  say this is a good thing and the Friends  of negative freedom are not that excited  about positive freedom but they can't  the net Friends of negative freedom  can't earn the victory by stamping on  the ground and saying that's not freedom  they after in the victory by saying that  what a society ought not to be providing  those things because those societies are  poor less prosperous there's less  Liberty of the kind they value and then  then then we're talking about substance  rather than that is about what society  should look like rather than about the  meaning of terms when we think about any  restriction on freedom how should we or  to what degree should we be considering  the possible negative externalities of  such a decision beyond sort of as you  say the specific substance of a decision  ok so there's a view let's call it so  2018 which is that a restriction on  freedom consists of blocking people  let's say through force from getting  where they want to go sure that's  inadequate there's two thousand nineteen  view is that people if people can't find  their way to the place where they want  to go e